# Refactor Safety

Before structural or cross-cutting refactors, produce a brief impact analysis. Prefer incremental refactors over full rewrites.

## Required impact checklist

| Area | Questions |
|------|-----------|
| **Tenant** | Does this change resolution, DB routing, or cookie/header policy? Affect all tenants? |
| **Mobile** | Break Expo auth, SecureStore, tenant header, portal WebView handoff, or push? |
| **Auth** | Change JWT/cookie flow, refresh, impersonation, or mobile-web-session? |
| **Notifications** | Affect in-app, email, browser, or Expo push delivery or deduplication? |
| **Realtime** | Change socket auth, room names, event names, or client subscriptions? |
| **Backward compat** | Will existing clients/API consumers break without coordinated deploy? |

## Refactor principles

- **Incremental** — Ship in small PRs; one concern per change when possible.
- **Feature flags / env gates** — Use existing env patterns before ripping out legacy paths.
- **Parity** — If backend changes, check web (`setupAxios`), mobile (`httpClient`), and docs.
- **No silent breaks** — Deprecate first; remove only when explicitly instructed.
- **Test tenant boundary** — Verify two tenants cannot read each other's data after refactor.

## When to stop and ask

- Replacing tenant middleware, auth middleware, or model routing.
- Renaming realtime events consumed by frontend/mobile.
- Changing upload access model or static file serving.
- Migrating from per-tenant DB to shared-schema (or reverse).

